FSTYP(8)                  BSD System Manager's Manual                 FSTYP(8)

NNAAMMEE
     ffssttyypp — determine filesystem type

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ffssttyypp [--ll] [--ss] [--uu] _s_p_e_c_i_a_l

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ffssttyypp utility is used to determine the filesystem type on a given de‐
     vice.  It can recognize BeFS (BeOS), ISO-9660, exFAT, Ext2, FAT, NTFS,
     and UFS filesystems.  When the --uu flag is specified, ffssttyypp also recog‐
     nizes certain additional metadata formats that cannot be handled using
     mount(8), such as geli(8) providers, and ZFS pools.

     The filesystem name is printed to the standard output as, respectively:
           befs
           cd9660
           exfat
           ext2fs
           geli
           hammer
           hammer2
           msdosfs
           ntfs
           ufs
           zfs

     Because ffssttyypp is built specifically to detect filesystem types, it dif‐
     fers from file(1) in several ways.  The output is machine-parsable,
     filesystem labels are supported, the utility runs sandboxed using
     capsicum(4), and does not try to recognize any file format other than
     filesystems.

     These options are available:

     --ll  In addition to filesystem type, print filesystem label if available.

     --ss  Ignore file type.  By default, ffssttyypp only works on regular files and
         disk-like device nodes.  Trying to read other file types might have
         unexpected consequences or hang indefinitely.

     --uu  Include filesystems and devices that cannot be mounted directly by
         mount(8).

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The ffssttyypp utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs or the
     filesystem type is not recognized.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     file(1), capsicum(4), autofs(5), geli(8), glabel(8), mount(8), zpool(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ffssttyypp command appeared in FreeBSD 10.2.

